  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nA fast moving wave will exit the area late this afternoon into the \nevening. A few sprinkles or patchy drizzle will be possible, but \nimpacts will be minimal. Otherwise the challenge will be stratus \ndevelopment with dissipation through the afternoon into the evening \nand then further redevelopment overnight into Tuesday morning. Even \nwith a few locations possibly falling into the upper 30s, a north \nwind of 5 to 10 mph and cloud cover will likely limit any frost \ndevelopment.\n\nAnother wave will move through NE on Tuesday and could bring a stray \nshowers into parts of northeast NE and northwest IA, but once again, \namounts will be light and impacts minimal. With some cloud cover \nand a cool northerly breeze, temperatures will remain around normal.\n\nWednesday will be mild and dry as upper level ridging builds ahead \nof the next stronger wave. High pressure at the surface will see \nlighter winds and temperatures around normal.\n\nThe next stronger wave moves into the area Wednesday night into \nThursday. Warm advection increases in the lower levels ahead of this \nwave Wednesday night which will bring the potential for some \nrainfall. The stronger warm advection is more likely across NE and \nexpect the better rainfall amounts to remain there as well. \nInstability looks very weak so thunderstorms are unlikely. Currently \nthe better chance for a more meaningful rainfall (0.25\" to 0.50\") is \nsouth of I-90 on Thursday during the day. The latest GEFS and EC \nEnsemble are indicating a 20-40 percent chance for more than a half \nan inch of rain south of I-90.\n\nSaturday morning will see cool surface high pressure settle south, \nbringing a chance for frost across the area. Some hints that \nsoutherly return flow may set up through the night so confidence on \nfrost development remains a bit low.\n\nA warming trend is looking more likely Sunday into early next week \nas faster westerly flow aloft transitions to upper level ridging \nthrough mid week. This will allow for a well mixed environment \nSunday and Monday, with southerly flow becoming more dominant mid to \nlate week. Some hints that a cool front may slip south on Monday, \nbut the effects on temperatures would not be real strong. This is \nstill 6-10 days out, so lots of room for changes, but for now \nlooking like a good chance for near to above normal temperatures \nSunday through the middle of next week.",
